## Solution

**Substitution.** Let $f(n) = 2^{n^2}\,g(n)$. Substituting into the recurrence:

$$2^{(n+2)^2}g(n+2) = 2^{2(n+2)}\cdot 2^{(n+1)^2}g(n+1) - 2^{4(n+1)}\cdot 2^{n^2}g(n) + n\cdot 2^{n^2}$$

Dividing through by $2^{n^2+4n+4}$ (the common power, since $(n+2)^2 = n^2+4n+4$):

$$g(n+2) = 2\,g(n+1) - g(n) + \frac{n}{16^{n+1}}$$

The homogeneous part $g(n+2)-2g(n+1)+g(n)=0$ is a second-difference equation with solution $A+Bn$. The initial conditions $f(0)=f(1)=0$ give $g(0)=g(1)=0$, so $A=B=0$ — the homogeneous solution vanishes entirely.

**Convolution formula.** With $h(k)=k/16^{k+1}$ and zero initial conditions:

$$g(n) = \sum_{k=0}^{n-2}(n-1-k)\,h(k) = \sum_{k=0}^{n-2}(n-1-k)\cdot\frac{k}{16^{k+1}}$$

Therefore:

$$f(n) = 2^{n^2}\sum_{k=0}^{n-2}(n-1-k)\cdot k\cdot 2^{-4(k+1)} = \sum_{k=0}^{n-2}(n-1-k)\cdot k\cdot 2^{n^2-4k-4}$$

**Verification on small cases.** $f(2)=0$, $f(3)=2$, $f(4)=544$ — all match direct computation from the recurrence.

**Reduction mod 13.** Since $\operatorname{ord}_{13}(2)=12$ (as $2^6\equiv -1$), we reduce exponents mod 12.

**$f(10)$:** $n^2-4=96$, and $96\equiv 0\pmod{12}$, so $2^{96-4k}\equiv 2^{-4k}\pmod{13}$, cycling through $\{1,9,3\}$ for $k\equiv 0,1,2\pmod 3$.

| $k$ | $(9{-}k)k$ | $2^{96-4k}\bmod 13$ | product mod 13 |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | 0 | 1 | 0 |
| 1 | 8 | 9 | 7 |
| 2 | 14 | 3 | 3 |
| 3 | 18 | 1 | 5 |
| 4 | 20 | 9 | 11 |
| 5 | 20 | 3 | 8 |
| 6 | 18 | 1 | 5 |
| 7 | 14 | 9 | 9 |
| 8 | 8 | 3 | 11 |

$$f(10)\equiv 0+7+3+5+11+8+5+9+11 = 49 \equiv 10 \pmod{13}$$

**$f(11)$:** $117\equiv 9\pmod{12}$, so exponents cycle $\{5,6,2\}$ (i.e.\ $2^9,2^5,2^1$).

| $k$ | $(10{-}k)k$ | power mod 13 | product mod 13 |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | 0 | 5 | 0 |
| 1 | 9 | 6 | 2 |
| 2 | 16 | 2 | 6 |
| 3 | 21 | 5 | 1 |
| 4 | 24 | 6 | 1 |
| 5 | 25 | 2 | 11 |
| 6 | 24 | 5 | 3 |
| 7 | 21 | 6 | 9 |
| 8 | 16 | 2 | 6 |
| 9 | 9 | 5 | 6 |

$$f(11)\equiv 0+2+6+1+1+11+3+9+6+6 = 45 \equiv 6 \pmod{13}$$

**$f(12)$:** $140\equiv 8\pmod{12}$, so exponents cycle $\{9,3,1\}$ (i.e.\ $2^8,2^4,2^0$).

| $k$ | $(11{-}k)k$ | power mod 13 | product mod 13 |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | 0 | 9 | 0 |
| 1 | 10 | 3 | 4 |
| 2 | 18 | 1 | 5 |
| 3 | 24 | 9 | 8 |
| 4 | 28 | 3 | 6 |
| 5 | 30 | 1 | 4 |
| 6 | 30 | 9 | 10 |
| 7 | 28 | 3 | 6 |
| 8 | 24 | 1 | 11 |
| 9 | 18 | 9 | 6 |
| 10 | 10 | 3 | 4 |

$$f(12)\equiv 0+4+5+8+6+4+10+6+11+6+4 = 64 \equiv 12 \pmod{13}$$

**Final answer:**

$$f(10)+f(11)+f(12)\equiv 10+6+12 = 28 \equiv \boxed{2}\pmod{13}$$

